PUBLIC RESPONSE: {C}

The scenario is governed by Ontario's Sale of Goods Act, s. 19 Rule 2: for specific goods that the seller must put into a deliverable state, property (and therefore risk) does not pass until the seller completes the work and gives the buyer notice that the goods are now in deliverable condition.  

On 2 June the MacBook was not deliverable (screen still had to be replaced), so risk remained with Jake. Jake's 5 June text stated only that the repair was "almost finished" and would be ready the next day; it did not constitute notice that the work had actually been completed. Consequently, the statutory notice requirement was never satisfied and risk never passed. Jake must therefore refund the purchase price.  

Choices A, B, D and E are inconsistent with the Act: A invokes irrelevant insurance considerations; B treats anticipatory notice as sufficient; D assumes risk passes only on physical delivery; and E ignores the "deliverable state" precondition.}

